The last viable native setting also fails the gate: at \(k=4,\ m=10n,\ n=256\), majority-initialized WalkSAT found a verified satisfying assignment on 6/8 seeds in 0.056–3.325 s (4,694–283,822 flips); two seeds hit the 400,000-flip cap. Because Track A requires zero successes for every attack, that is disqualifying. Track B cannot rescue it: the paper gives no separate compact invariant—tallying the planted sign bias alone costs 10,240 literal inspections, and it still needs thousands of repair flips, far beyond the 300-operation cap. I’m recording the Step-0 rejection with these measurements and the paper’s own recommended-size mismatch.
